            "a": "TRANSFUSION-ASSOCIATED CIRCULATORY OVERLOAD (TACO) with secondary acute hepatic congestion/acute cholestasis — massive rapid transfusion causes intravascular volume overload → pulmonary edema (if pulmonary involvement) + hepatic congestion (right heart strain from volume overload backups blood into liver) → acute cholestasis/cirrhosis worsening; MANAGEMENT: STOP transfusion immediately, aggressive diuresis (furosemide IV), oxygen if pulmonary component, slow future transfusions (target <1 unit per 1-2 hours), consider transfusion with furosemide co-administration for future",
            "rationale": "TACO occurs when transfusion volume exceeds patient's cardiac ability to handle (especially elderly, renal failure, cardiac disease). This patient's acute cholestasis WITHOUT obstruction suggests hepatic congestion from RV strain (volume overload effect). Key difference from TRALI: TACO has elevated LAP + pulmonary edema (vs. TRALI normal LAP non-cardiogenic), TACO responds to diuretics (vs. TRALI contraindicated). Management: (1) STOP transfusion, (2) Aggressive diuresis (furosemide 40-80mg IV), (3) Oxygen support, (4) Elevation of head of bed, (5) Vasopressor support if hypotensive (unusual but may be needed), (6) For future transfusions: slower rates (1 unit over 2 hours), give furosemide 20mg IV after each unit, (7) Consider transfusion alternatives (phlebotomy pre-transfusion, pharmacologic agents, iron supplement). Prognosis: good with diuresis; acute cholestasis resolves as hepatic congestion improves."
