            "q": "A 54-year-old with severe autoimmune hemolytic anemia (Hgb 4.2, retic 28%, LDH 2200) unresponsive to high-dose steroids + IVIG receives emergency transfusion (2 units PRBC). Within 20 minutes: sudden worsening dyspnea, hypotension (BP 88/52), chest pain, troponin 0.18. EKG shows new ST depression. What is the UNIQUE transfusion complication in AIHA?",
            "a": "ACUTE HEMOLYTIC TRANSFUSION REACTION in AIHA patient (transfused RBCs immediately attacked by patient's warm antibodies which coat ALL RBCs including donor RBCs) → acute massive hemolysis WORSE than baseline — differential diagnosis can be confused with AHTR from alloimmunization, but key finding is DAT + current transfusion worsens hemolysis despite blood bank type & cross; MANAGEMENT: STOP transfusion immediately, aggressive IV hydration (myoglobin/hemoglobin-induced AKI risk with Hgb 4.2 baseline + acute additional hemolysis), may need transfusion-free approach (support with IV fluids, oxygen, consider exchange transfusion if life-threatening, escalate immunosuppression instead of transfusing)",
            "rationale": "UNIQUE problem in AIHA: warm antibodies coat ALL RBCs (patient's own + any donor RBCs) → transfusion paradoxically WORSENS hemolysis. Mechanism: (1) AIHA = patient's warm autoantibodies attacking own RBCs (DAT positive), (2) Transfusion adds donor RBCs which are ALSO attacked by same warm antibodies (panreactive antibodies), (3) Result: acute hemolysis accelerates, Hgb may drop FASTER than without transfusion. This is why transfusion is CONTRAINDICATED in stable AIHA (unless life-threatening with Hgb <5-6). Management: (1) STOP transfusion, (2) Aggressive IV hydration (myoglobin-induced AKI risk), (3) ESCALATE immunosuppression: consider exchange transfusion (removes cold plasma with antibodies while adding RBCs), or intensive plasmapheresis, or rituximab, (4) Transfusion-free approach preferred in AIHA (IV hydration, oxygen, pharmacologic support), (5) If Hgb <5 (life-threatening): exchange transfusion or partial exchange may help (reduces antibody concentration while adding RBCs). Prognosis: depends on immunosuppressive response; transfusion often makes things WORSE."
