            "q": "A 62-year-old with history of multiple prior transfusions (for chronic anemia from myelodysplasia) receives 3 units PRBC for symptomatic anemia. Day 4 post-transfusion: develops fever (38.4°C), malaise, jaundice (bilirubin 2.8), elevated LFTs (ALT 280, AST 340), abdominal discomfort. Labs: negative hepatitis serology (HAV, HBV, HCV), negative blood cultures. What is the diagnosis specific to transfusion recipient with multiple prior transfusions?",
            "a": "TRANSFUSION-TRANSMITTED CYTOMEGALOVIRUS (CMV) infection or TRANSFUSION-ASSOCIATED IRON OVERLOAD HEPATOPATHY (secondary hemochromatosis from chronic transfusions causing cirrhosis progression) — differentiate: CMV would have other systemic symptoms (pneumonitis, retinitis risk in immunocompromised), hepatopathy would show ferritin elevated + TSAT elevated (iron overload markers); MANAGEMENT: if CMV → ganciclovir/valganciclovir (or foscarnet if resistant), if iron overload → chelation therapy (deferoxamine IV, deferasirox oral), hepatology consultation, assess cirrhosis burden (ultrasound, elastography), monitor for hepatic decompensation",
            "rationale": "Chronically transfused patients at risk for: (1) CMV from blood products (risk 10-15% with multiple transfusions), (2) Iron overload cirrhosis (each unit PRBC delivers ~250mg iron; chronic transfusion → 10-15g iron accumulation → cirrhosis). This patient's presentation (fever, jaundice, elevated LFTs, negative viral serology) suggests either CMV or iron overload. Management: (1) Check ferritin + TSAT (if elevated = iron overload), (2) CMV serology/PCR (if positive = CMV infection), (3) If CMV: ganciclovir 5mg/kg IV BID or valganciclovir 900mg PO daily, (4) If iron overload: chelation therapy (deferoxamine 20-30mg/kg IV daily or deferasirox 20-40mg/kg daily), (5) Hepatology consultation (assess cirrhosis grade, HCC risk), (6) Hepatitis serology ALREADY negative (rules out HAV/HBV/HCV transmission). Prognosis: CMV infection responds to antivirals; iron overload-related cirrhosis is progressive (median survival cirrhosis ~10 years untreated)."
