            "q": "A 63-year-old with multiple prior transfusions (chronic anemia from refractory anemia with excess blasts — RAEB) develops PURE RED CELL APLASIA (Hgb 5.2, retic 0%, WBC normal, Plt normal, bone marrow shows selective erythroid hypoplasia). DAT negative. He received transfusions 2 weeks ago. What is the RARE immune complication of transfusion?",
            "a": "TRANSFUSION-ASSOCIATED PURE RED CELL APLASIA (TRPRCA) from alloimmunization to RBC antigen triggering selective destruction of patient's OWN erythroid progenitors (not just transfused RBCs) — mechanism: minor RBC antigen from prior transfusion stimulated alloimmunization → immune response cross-reacts with patient's own RBCs creating auto-immune destruction; DIAGNOSTIC clue: retic 0% (bone marrow not responding) + DAT negative (patient's own RBCs NOT coated with antibody, so DAT negative) = paradoxical immune attack on own cells without detectable antibodies; MANAGEMENT: STOP all transfusions (transfusions worsen condition by re-stimulating immune response), consider immunosuppression (cyclosporine, corticosteroids), IVIG to reduce immune activation, intravenous immunoglobulin high-dose, stem cell transplantation if immunosuppression fails (definitive therapy), rarely resolves spontaneously over months",
            "rationale": "TRPRCA is RARE (1 per 1,000,000 transfusions) but DEVASTATING. Pathophysiology: (1) Prior transfusion with minor RBC antigen → alloimmunization, (2) Antibodies cross-react with patient's OWN erythroid progenitors (molecular mimicry), (3) T-cell mediated destruction of patient's RBC production, (4) Result: selective red cell aplasia (WBC, Plt normal because immune attack specific to RBC progenitors). Key distinction: DAT NEGATIVE (attacks developing RBCs in marrow, not circulating RBCs — so DAT on mature RBCs negative). Management: (1) STOP transfusions (each transfusion re-stimulates immune response worsening condition), (2) Immunosuppression: cyclosporine (most effective, response rate ~60%), corticosteroids (prednisone 1-2mg/kg daily), (3) IVIG 2g/kg (reduces immune activation), (4) Expect slow recovery (weeks to months if responding to immunosuppression), (5) If immunosuppression fails: stem cell transplant (only definitive cure), (6) Supportive transfusions ONLY if Hgb <5 (risky but necessary for survival while awaiting immunosuppressive response). Prognosis: 50-60% respond to cyclosporine ± corticosteroids, others require SCT."
