            "a": "CONFLUENCE of multiple transfusion complications: (1) TRALI (bilateral infiltrates, non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema), (2) TACO component (possible volume overload contributing to respiratory distress), (3) BACTERIAL CONTAMINATION (fever + elevated WBC suggests sepsis), (4) MASSIVE TRANSFUSION COAGULOPATHY (DIC from multiple triggers: massive transfusion dilutes clotting factors, tissue damage releases thromboplastin, sepsis activates coagulation), (5) ACIDOSIS from shock/resuscitation; EMERGENCY MANAGEMENT: (1) ECMO/ECLS STAT (only intervention addressing respiratory failure + circulatory support + anticoagulation simultaneously), (2) Broad-spectrum antibiotics (assume bacterial contamination until proven otherwise), (3) DIC management (FFP, platelets, cryoprecipitate for coagulopathy), (4) Hold all further transfusions except as supportive care during ECMO setup, (5) ICU-level care mandatory",
            "rationale": "'PERFECT STORM': multiple catastrophes converging simultaneously. Each complication worsens others: (1) TRALI pulmonary edema worsens oxygenation, (2) DIC prevents effective hemostasis (bleeding everywhere), (3) Sepsis amplifies SIRS response worsening ARDS + coagulopathy, (4) Acidosis → arrhythmias, myocardial depression, (5) AKI from hypotension + myoglobin (rhabdomyolysis from massive transfusion + shock). This patient is at EXTREME mortality risk (>70%). Management: (1) ECMO/ECLS EMERGENTLY (no other intervention can support respiratory failure + cardiogenic shock + manage anticoagulation + oxygenate simultaneously), (2) STAT blood product cultures (identify contaminating organism), (3) Aggressive antibiotics: cover cold-growing organisms (if contamination) + standard sepsis coverage, (4) Blood bank notification (massive transfusion reaction — reportable), (5) DIC management: FFP + platelets + cryoprecipitate (goal: Fib >100, PT <20, aPTT <60), (6) Acid-base management: sodium bicarbonate, mechanical ventilation optimization, (7) Renal support: dialysis prepared (AKI likely permanent given sepsis + DIC + hypotension), (8) Vasopressor support (dobutamine, norepinephrine), (9) Source control if ongoing hemorrhage (OR). Prognosis: mortality 70-80% even with aggressive ECMO + support."
