            "q": "A 63-year-old with SEVERE HYPONATREMIA (Na 94) from SIADH is treated with HYPERTONIC SALINE (3%) aggressively (correcting at 8-10 mEq/L per hour). After 36 hours: Na corrected to 120. THEN develops ACUTE BRAINSTEM SYMPTOMS (locked-in syndrome, no movement except eyes, unable to speak). MRI shows OSMOTIC DEMYELINATION SYNDROME (ODS) with central pontine myelinolysis. What is the COMPLICATION of HIS treatment?",
            "a": "OSMOTIC DEMYELINATION SYNDROME (ODS) from TOO-RAPID sodium correction (correcting >8-10 mEq/L per 24 hours causes demyelination injury to pons + extrapontine structures) — this patient's aggressive correction 8-10 mEq/L per HOUR over 36 hours = likely >20 mEq/L total correction = EXCESSIVE and caused ODS; MANAGEMENT: STOP sodium correction immediately, allow Na to DRIFT BACK DOWN slowly (goal: decrease correction rate to <8 mEq/L per 24 hours going forward), supportive care (locked-in syndrome is severe neurologic injury), physical therapy/rehabilitation (some patients recover partially over months with intensive therapy), prognosis poor (mortality 10-15% even with supportive care)",
            "rationale": "ODS is IATROGENIC complication from treatment error. Mechanism: (1) Severe chronic hyponatremia (Na 94) → brain adapts by decreasing intracellular osmolytes (taurine, sorbitol, myo-inositol), (2) Rapid sodium correction → osmotic gradient reverses → water leaves brain cells → demyelination of pons + extrapontine white matter, (3) Locked-in syndrome = severe brainstem injury. KEY: prevent ODS during treatment (never correct >8 mEq/L per 24 hours). Management of established ODS: (1) STOP further sodium correction, (2) Allow Na to drift back down (slower re-equilibration), (3) Supportive care (mechanical ventilation possible if locked-in), (4) Rehabilitation (some recovery possible over months), (5) Prognosis very poor (mortality 10-15%, survivors often have permanent neurologic disability). This patient's aggressive treatment caused WORSE complication than original hyponatremia."
